preConditionHook:
// THE DAY'S OPENING PRICE AS THE TARGET. After a trend-from-the-open sell-off
// reverses, Brooks's measured move up is the open-to-low distance projected
// from the low -- and since the low IS the projection's anchor, that target
// resolves exactly to the session's OPENING PRICE. So the mechanized rule is
// one price held across the session, not a measured move at all: exit the long
// when the bar's high reaches the day's open, the short when the low reaches
// it.
//
// SESSION MODEL per the standing decision: a session starts when the bar's
// wall-clock close date changes -- EL's date > date[1], mirrored as
// day_of(ctx.Time(0)) > day_of(ctx.Time(1)) -- and the session's open is that
// bar's open, held in a local until the next date change. OpenD(0) is VERIFIED
// and would give the same number in the twin, but it has no engine
// counterpart, so both twins carry the session in a variable and stay
// structurally identical; that is the choice SessionMoveExhaustsDailyRange
// records.
//
// haveSess IS THE WARM-UP GUARD. Before the first date change of the run the
// variable still holds its initial 0, and 0 as a price sits under every real
// price, so an unguarded long would exit on its entry bar. EL variables start
// at 0 and hold it until first assigned, so this mirrors EL's behaviour rather
// than picking the more defensible number.
//
// offsetTicks (default 0, Brooks's literal level) pushes the target that many
// ticks BEYOND the open -- up for the long, down for the short. Ticks are
// written division-first, ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale() computed once into
// tickSz; both are per-symbol constants (VERIFIED, one change print per chart,
// at bar 1).
//
// THE PLACEMENT TRAP, as with MovingAverageTarget: this is written for a long
// entered BELOW the day's open, so a long entered above it exits on its first
// bar. A placement concern, not a change to the condition.
//
// INTRADAY ONLY. On a daily or 1440-minute series every bar is its own
// session, so sessOpen is this bar's own open and the rule degenerates to "the
// bar traded back to its own open".
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the
// generator only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration
// is a redefinition when this rule is placed twice in one strategy.
// Every one is fully assigned each bar before it is read.
tickSz = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
if (day_of(ctx.Time(0)) > day_of(ctx.Time(1))) {
    sessOpen = open[0];
    haveSess = true;
}
